Make the Cloudflare fallback manual-only, the way lesbistrology already does it

Your git integration has stopped deploying in **more than one place**, so this
gives FCPM a fallback you can actually reach for — built to the shape
`DiscoveryWritten/lesbistrology` has been running for a month, rather than a
second invention of my own.

## It is not just FCPM

| repo | main last moved | last deployment |
|---|---|---|
| `fcpublicmedia.org` | 2026-09-10 01:55Z | 2026-09-10 01:55Z, then a failed build at 07:38Z |
| `lesbistrology` | **2026-08-29** (#101) | **2026-08-11 23:44Z** |

lesbistrology merged to `main` on 29 August and never deployed. Same silent
stop, twelve days earlier. Whatever this is, it is account-shaped, not
repository-shaped — which matches your read that Cloudflare damaged a batch of
connections.

## I was wrong about the root directory

I suspected `/` was being read as the filesystem root. **It is not**, and your
own `docs/deploy-cloudflare.md` says so plainly:

> | Root directory | `/` | `wrangler.jsonc` lives there. |

lesbistrology deploys successfully with exactly that. Leave FCPM's alone.

**What the log actually says.** `Detected the following tools from environment:`
is empty, nothing cached, and `Could not locate Gemfile` is *bundler's own*
error — so Ruby was present and ran. An image that works, in a directory with
nothing in it. That is an empty or partial **checkout**, not a wrong path, and
it points at the GitHub App's access to the repository, which is what you are
already reauthorizing.

The Gemfile is fine, for the record: `Gemfile`, `.ruby-version`,
`.tool-versions`, `_config.yml` and `wrangler.jsonc` are all at the root of
`main`.

## The change

**`deploy.yml` loses its Cloudflare steps.** They are now
`.github/workflows/deploy-cloudflare.yml`, **`workflow_dispatch` only**.

This fixes something I built badly two days ago. My version ran on `push` and
was kept harmless by the token being absent — which made adding an organization
secret into a way to start publishing the same site twice without meaning to. I
even had to warn you not to create `CLOUDFLARE_PAGES_TOKEN`. **Safety that
depends on a credential not existing is a landmine with a note on it.**

lesbistrology solved this properly and I should have copied it the first time:

> It is manual-only so it cannot double-deploy against the Git integration.

Now the trigger is the guard, so the secret can exist for the broker's sake
without arming anything.

Also adopted from that repo: `cloudflare/wrangler-action@v3` instead of raw
`npx wrangler`, and a `concurrency` group so two deploys of one Worker cannot
race.

One thing added that lesbistrology does not have — a refusal:

```yaml
test -f _site/index.html || exit 1
```

Publishing an empty or half-built directory is worse than not publishing: it
replaces a working site with a broken one and reports success.

## README

The section now describes the manual path honestly, and gains **"When the git
build fails"** — both failures seen so far, what each log looks like, and the
explicit note that `/` is correct so nobody re-derives my wrong guess. Plus the
reassurance that neither failure takes the site down, because the last good
deployment keeps serving.

## Verified

- Both workflows parse (`YAML.load_file`).
- `deploy-cloudflare.yml` triggers on `workflow_dispatch` and nothing else.
- `deploy.yml` is back to build → Azure, with the build gate on pull requests
  unchanged.
- Deployment histories and merge times above read from `wrangler deployments
  list` and `gh`, not inferred.

## To actually use it

Needs `CLOUDFLARE_PAGES_TOKEN` as an org secret — the same token type item 1.2
of `PAYMENTS-CHECKLIST.md` wants for the broker, so making one now serves both.
Account-scoped, no zone.
